PENDING PATCH — the resume cache can answer "covered: true" without looking
Written 2026-09-12. NOT APPLIED, NOT TESTED (sandbox down all session).
SEVERITY: HIGH — it has already produced one false DEMOTE (ORDER, 2026-09-12).

THE DEFECT
  detect-cliff-cluster.js / outflowsWithRecipients begins:

    if (c0?.done) return { byDay, covered: true, oldest, pages: 0, resumed: true };

  `done` means "this fetch once reached back to the untilDate it was given". It says
  NOTHING about the RECENT end of the range. Every entry in
  data/cliff-fetch-cache.json is `"done":true,"at":"2026-09-05T17:3x"` with newest
  day 2026-09-05. When pollCliffWatch asked on 2026-09-12 whether ORDER's vault had
  emitted in 09-07..09-12, it was handed that cache: covered:true, 0 pages, no
  network, and no days in the window. Verdict: DEMOTE at ratio 0, recipients 0.

  This is the "we did not look is never a verdict" rule failing through a new door.
  The `covered` flag was not bypassed — the cache HANDED it a true value having
  fetched nothing. windowObserved() is satisfied by a lie it cannot detect.

  Introduced 2026-09-05 with the resumable cache (added so L3's 120-page proxy could
  not eat a budget slice). Same day, same file.

FIX 1 — a cache may only satisfy a request whose window it actually covers
  `done` must be qualified by the NEWEST day the cache holds. A resume is valid only
  when the cache extends at least to the end of the range being asked about.

  Requires the cache to record its newest day (it records `oldest` only), and the
  caller to say how recent a read it needs. Sketch — REVIEW, do not paste blind:

    // cache entry gains: newest (YYYY-MM-DD), written alongside oldest
    // signature gains: needUpTo (YYYY-MM-DD) — the last day the caller will score
    const fresh = c0?.newest && needUpTo && c0.newest >= needUpTo;
    if (c0?.done && fresh) return { byDay, covered: true, oldest, pages: 0, resumed: true };
    // a done-but-stale cache is a STARTING POINT, not an answer: keep byDay, drop
    // `done`, and page forward from the newest end before returning covered.

  Until the forward-paging path exists, the CONSERVATIVE version is correct and one
  line: treat a stale cache as no cache.

    if (c0?.done && c0?.newest && needUpTo && c0.newest < needUpTo) { /* refetch */ }

FIX 2 — pollCliffWatch must state what it needs
  It scores [cliff, cliff + windowDays]. It must pass needUpTo = that end date, and
  must treat `resumed: true` with an insufficient `newest` as NOT covered — i.e. the
  existing `if (!f.covered) continue;` keeps working, because covered becomes false.

FIX 3 — a DEMOTE must carry its own strength, like a CONFIRM does
  Raised in review 2026-09-12 and correct independently of the cache bug: the
  compound machinery (chanceRate, replayRate, binomial tail) is attached only to
  CONFIRMs. A DEMOTE is just a DEMOTE, which matters most on a WEAK falsifier.
    - ORDER: chanceRate 0.58, so an empty window is a ~42% event under the null.
      Near-noise even if it had been observed.
    - ENA: 5,148,798 vs a 13-month minimum of 6,413,805 — P(next draw is the
      smallest of 14) ≈ 1/14 ≈ 0.07 under exchangeability, and 20% below the prior
      floor. Genuinely informative.
  Carry the figure into BOTH the overlay entry and the demote message, so
  re-promotion logic and a human reading the DM can tell those two apart. Suggested
  line: "Strength: this window is empty ~42% of the time by chance (chanceRate 0.58)
  — weak evidence, confirm before acting." / for a break below an all-time floor,
  state the rank probability instead.

IMMEDIATE, BEFORE ANY OF THE ABOVE
  1. Treat data/cadence-watch.json's ORDER demotion and its cliffs entry
     ORDER:2026-09-07 as VOID. They record a verdict from a fetch that never ran.
  2. Do NOT hand-edit them while the bot is running — pollCliffWatch re-reads and
     rewrites this file each cycle. Stop the bot first, or fix the code and let a
     fresh evaluation overwrite.
  3. The `cliffs` stamp BLOCKS re-evaluation of that cliff (`due` filters on
     `!st.cliffs[key]`), so this will NOT self-correct once the cache is fixed. The
     stamp must be cleared for 2026-09-07 to be scored honestly.
  4. Deleting data/cliff-fetch-cache.json is safe on its own (the code handles
     absence) and makes every future cliff read fetch fresh — but it does not undo
     items 1-3.

INCIDENT 2026-09-12 — I CORRUPTED data/cadence-watch.json BY HAND-EDITING IT
  Attempting to mark ORDER's false demotion `void`, I wrote a JSON object and omitted
  its closing brace. The bot's loadWatchState() caught the parse error, returned the
  empty default `{ months: {}, demotions: {} }`, and the pollers re-derived ALL state
  from scratch at 13:56-13:58.

  WHAT WAS LOST
    - the void annotation itself, entirely;
    - every original verdict timestamp (ENA's demote was 2026-09-10T19:05, now
      13:58; ORDER's cliff stamp was 01:58, now 13:56);
    - ENA's `notified: true`, which meant the DEMOTE DM was almost certainly SENT TO
      THE OPERATOR A SECOND TIME. If a duplicate ENA demotion notice arrived on the
      12th, that is this, not a new event.

  WHAT SURVIVED, and it is worth something: the re-derivation reproduced every
  verdict EXACTLY — EIGEN 2026-08 CONFIRM 0.976, MOVE 2026-09 CONFIRM 2.048 /
  19,376,705, ENA 2026-09 DEMOTE largestSeen 5,148,798. Unintended, but it is real
  evidence that the watch is deterministic over the same inputs.

  DEFECT THIS EXPOSED — loadWatchState() TREATS A CORRUPT FILE AS "NO HISTORY".
    try { return JSON.parse(readFileSync(STATE_FILE, 'utf8')); }
    catch { return { months: {}, demotions: {} }; }
  A silent, total reset of verdict history on any parse failure — a typo, a torn
  write, disk corruption — with NO log line and no operator alert. This time the
  windows were recent enough to re-derive; a month later they would not have been,
  and the history would have been gone with nothing said. Same class as everything
  else in this file: a failure that leaves no trace.
  FIX: on parse failure, rename the bad file to `cadence-watch.corrupt-<ts>.json`,
  log `[OPERATOR]`, and — since an empty state causes re-notification — refuse to
  send demote DMs on the first cycle after a reset.

  THE LESSON, NOW DEMONSTRATED RATHER THAN ARGUED: hand-editing a bot-owned file does
  not work. Not "races sometimes" — it destroyed the file on the first attempt and
  silently discarded every verdict in it. The annotation write path
  (`node annotate-verdict.js ORDER 2026-09-07 --void --reason "..."`) is not a queue
  item to get to eventually; it is the ONLY safe way to record a correction, and its
  absence is why this was attempted by hand at all. It must: take the lock the way
  promote-unlock.js does for unlocks.json, validate the JSON it produces before
  renaming it into place, and refuse to run while the field it writes is unknown to
  the reader that must honour it.
  Third occurrence of this hazard in the project's history (regime tags clobbered in
  v0.13.1, the outcomes.json tear in v0.17, this).

SWEEP — "could this flag be true while the thing it claims never happened?"
Run 2026-09-12 across every .js in the repo. The result is narrower than feared:

  DERIVED, arithmetic at the point of use — these cannot lie:
    detect-cliff-cluster.js:63,77   covered: true  <- spanCovered(oldest, untilDate)
    cadence-watch.js:151            covered: true  <- spanCovered(oldest, untilDate)
    detect-cliff-cluster.js:62,76   covered: true  <- !next_page_params, history exhausted
    cadence-watch.js:150            covered: true  <- !next_page_params, history exhausted

  ASSERTED, set alongside the action rather than computed from its product:
    detect-cliff-cluster.js:47      covered: true  <- if (c0?.done)      *** THE BUG ***

  FIVE of the six `covered: true` returns in this codebase are already arithmetic.
  Exactly ONE is an assertion, and it is the one that produced a false verdict.
  That is the whole finding, and it makes the durable fix small: route line 47
  through the SAME function as the others, extended to bound the recent end.

    // covered becomes a pure function of what was actually fetched vs what is scored
    export function rangeCovers(oldest, coveredTo, from, to) {
      return !!oldest && !!coveredTo && oldest < from && coveredTo >= to;
    }

  *** THE SECOND ARGUMENT IS NOT "THE NEWEST ROW FOUND". ***
  Caught in review 2026-09-12, before this was applied, and it inverts the very
  failure the fix exists for. If `coveredTo` were derived from the newest TRANSACTION
  in the fetch, then a vault that genuinely STOPPED emitting has nothing in or after
  the window, so coveredTo < to, the window reads UNCOVERED, and the row goes PENDING
  FOREVER instead of demoting. The stale-cache case and the silent-vault case become
  indistinguishable — and the one we most need to detect is the one that never
  resolves. A row stuck PENDING is this project's worst failure shape: silent, and
  indistinguishable from health.

  `coveredTo` must be the BOUNDARY THE FETCH REACHED, not a property of the data it
  found. The readers page BACKWARDS FROM THE HEAD, so a completed fetch covers
  [oldest-boundary, fetch-start]. Therefore:

    coveredTo = the wall-clock time the fetch STARTED (or the head block at that
                moment), NOT the newest row, and NOT the time it finished.

  Fetch-START, not fetch-end: paging takes seconds to minutes and the head advances
  while it runs, so claiming coverage to fetch-END overclaims by the duration of the
  fetch and could miss a transaction that landed mid-page. Fetch-start under-claims
  slightly, which is the safe direction.

  Then: a fresh fetch of a silent vault reports coveredTo = now, covers the window,
  finds zero, and DEMOTES correctly. A cache written 2026-09-05 reports
  coveredTo = 2026-09-05, fails to cover a 09-12 window, and REFETCHES. The two cases
  separate cleanly, which is the entire point.

  The cache already stores what is needed: each entry carries `at`. It must be
  re-recorded as the fetch START (it is currently written at persist time) and read
  as the coverage boundary rather than as a note about when the file was touched.

  SYMMETRY NOTE, because it explains why this was easy to get wrong: `oldest` is
  likewise NOT sufficient on its own — it is a data property used as a proxy for the
  backward boundary, and it is only sound in combination with the history-exhausted
  check (`!next_page_params`). Either you paged PAST the target, or you ran out of
  history; both mean full coverage below. The recent end had no such pairing at all,
  which is how an assertion slipped in.

  Then a cache ending 2026-09-05 asked about 2026-09-07..09-12 returns false BY
  ARITHMETIC. There is nothing left to lie with, and `done` stops being load-bearing:
  it becomes a resume hint, not an attestation.

  EARNED — set only on evidence of the thing itself:
    cadence-watch.js:199,256   notified = true  <- only `if (ids.length)`, i.e. only
                               when Telegram returned message ids. Correct.
    src/core/pulse.js          notePulse()      <- called after a successful fetch.
    telemetry.js:125           done = ids.length || !hasRecipients(false)
                               Can be true without delivery, but ONLY when there is
                               nobody to deliver to — and the claim it makes is "this
                               digest is settled for today", not "it was delivered".
                               Deliberate and documented; passes the test in spirit.

  MISSING ENTIRELY — worse than a false attestation, because there is no record
  either way and no retry:
    pollCliffWatch             writes st.demotions[sym] with NO `notified` field and
                               broadcasts with `.catch(() => [])`. Already logged as
                               FIX 3's sibling; the cadence path at :256 is the model.

  MIDDLE GROUND, noted not actioned:
    promoteRow -> verified: true is an assertion, but verifiedRowProblems re-derives
    its preconditions at every boot, so a lie cannot survive a restart.
    sourceFetchedAt copies the index file's own capture time; re-ingesting an OLD
    index correctly carries the OLD timestamp, and the staleness ladder then fires.
    Honest by construction.

MISSING CAPABILITY — NO VERDICT CAN EVER BE RE-SCORED (general, not ORDER's)
  `due` filters on `!st.cliffs[key]`, and the cadence path similarly skips a month
  once `rec.action !== 'DEMOTE' || rec.notified`. So ANY verdict later found wrong —
  for any reason, not just this cache bug — is permanent. The only way to correct one
  is to hand-edit a file the bot rewrites every cycle, which is tampering, leaves no
  trace, and races the poller.
  What is missing is a supported path:
    - `node rescore.js ORDER 2026-09-07 --reason "resume cache returned covered:true
      without fetching (2026-09-12)"` — clears the stamp and any demotion it caused,
      and APPENDS to a rescore log rather than silently deleting.
    - the row's history keeps both: the original verdict, and the correction with its
      reason. A correction recorded as a correction, not as an absence.
    - refuse to rescore without a reason string, the same discipline as
      clusterSpec.basis and toleranceBasis: the number is the fact, the reason is why
      anyone should believe the second reading over the first.
  Until it exists, corrections require stopping the bot — which is itself an argument
  for building it.

TEST PLAN
  - Unit: a cache entry with newest < needUpTo must NOT satisfy the request. Assert
    covered === false, and assert pages > 0 on the refetch path.
  - Regression: the ORDER window 2026-09-07..2026-09-12 re-scored against a live
    fetch, and the verdict compared with what the stale cache produced. If the vault
    really did emit in that window, this is a confirmed false demotion and the
    fixture should pin exactly that case.
  - The rule to assert, not the shape: "no verdict may be produced from a fetch whose
    covered range does not include the scored window."
